      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view base rate/reference class is a short monthly persistence model on the same official FNS national Persons variant. The latest level is 36.562128 million in May 2026, with seven observed month-to-month changes from October 2025 through May 2026: -1.096770, -0.791992, -0.655765, -0.605010, -0.415971, -0.452556, and -0.516468 million."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level effect starts at 36.562128 million. Momentum effect uses the more recent four-change average from Jan-May 2026, -0.497501 million per month, rather than the full seven-change mean of -0.647790 because the earliest FY2026 drops look unusually front-loaded. One-off/policy mechanism is set to 0.000 million because the draft did not establish a concrete public policy adjustment separate from the observed trend."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = latest official May 2026 level of 36.562128 million; historical sample = Oct 2025-May 2026 same-table Persons values listed above. Successive changes are -1.096770, -0.791992, -0.655765, -0.605010, -0.415971, -0.452556, and -0.516468 million; their sample standard deviation gives sigma = 0.235716 million. Point = 36.562128 + 4*(-0.497501) + 0.000 = 34.572124 million, rounded to 34.572. The one-month 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.235716 = 0.301717 million; for a four-month horizon I scale by sqrt(4), giving 0.603434 million. Bounds are 34.572124 - 0.603434 = 33.968690 and 34.572124 + 0.603434 = 35.175558, rounded to 33.969 and 35.176. The interval reflects recent monthly-change dispersion scaled over the four-month horizon and does not add a separate policy-adjustment term."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk is that the early FY2026 enrollment drop stabilizes quickly, state backlogs or recertifications raise the September count, or disaster-related reporting adds participants; that would land above the interval if September exceeds 35.176 million. Downside risk is faster eligibility loss, stronger work-requirement implementation, or administrative churn continuing near the Oct-Jan decline pace; that would land below the interval if September is under 33.969 million. The outside the interval cases are therefore a clear stabilization above 35.176 or an accelerated drop below 33.969."
      },
      {
